WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.000
[MUSIC]

00:00:11.209 --> 00:00:13.144
Ratan, thank you so much for joining us
today.

00:00:13.144 --> 00:00:14.479
It's an honor to speak with you.

00:00:14.479 --> 00:00:15.153
And I know I speak for

00:00:15.153 --> 00:00:17.777
many here in the audience when I say that
we've been looking forward to this for

00:00:17.777 --> 00:00:18.350
question time.

00:00:19.930 --> 00:00:20.490
&gt;&gt; Thank you Jones.

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:23.970
&gt;&gt; I wanted to start at the beginning of
your career.

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:27.070
As the Dean mentioned, you were educated
at Cornell as an architect and

00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:29.280
a structural engineer and

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:32.860
planned to spend the rest of your career
in the United States.

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:36.630
But you returned to India, starting on the
shop floor, and

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:40.240
I wonder what that was like as a young man
returning from the United States to India.

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:47.190
&gt;&gt; Well, quite frankly, it was a, as much
a cultural shock.

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:51.860
I'd been away for ten years, and things
had changed, and

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:55.700
yet, they had not changed, and the
difference was quite substantive.

00:00:57.730 --> 00:01:04.080
In addition to which I didn't return to
India to spend time on the shop floor.

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:08.790
So, I, I didn't really understand what was
being done,

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:13.983
and several times during that period of
time I thought of going back to

00:01:13.983 --> 00:01:19.180
the US and pursuing my career as an
architect.

00:01:21.370 --> 00:01:24.610
&gt;&gt; And you spent several years as the Dean
mentioned,

00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:28.150
moving around different parts of the Tata
organization.

00:01:28.150 --> 00:01:31.480
Were you surprised when you got the call
from your uncle in 1991 to

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:33.910
be chairman of, of Tata?

00:01:33.910 --> 00:01:34.690
&gt;&gt; Yes I was.

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:41.460
Mainly because the remaining contenders
who are, not many,

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:46.636
handful of contenders for that position
from his own team of,

00:01:46.636 --> 00:01:54.960
of people and it happened very suddenly
and, and

00:01:54.960 --> 00:02:00.310
you, you begin to feel that leader he was
there for 50 plus years as chairman.

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:05.615
And you start to believe that somebody's
immortal and

00:02:05.615 --> 00:02:10.510
that he's, he's going to be there
perpetually, so when he calls you and

00:02:10.510 --> 00:02:14.810
says this is what he wants to do, it
shakes you up.

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:16.880
And suddenly you feel all alone.

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:17.380
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:02:18.770 --> 00:02:22.700
And you've faced quite a formidable
challenge as the, as the Dean mentioned.

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:26.740
When you took over as CEO, the
organization, Tata, was large and

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:31.590
sprawling, with independent business
heads, that regarded their own, their own

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:36.190
business as their own personal fiefdoms
didn't necessarily play together well.

00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:38.980
And you were in an economy that was
changing rapidly.

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:43.470
And your uncle was considered a titan of
Indian business and very large shoes for

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:45.660
a young CEO to fill.

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:49.440
How did you go about establishing your
credibility as a leader in those

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:49.960
early days?

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:53.480
&gt;&gt; I had, I had two, two problems.

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:58.320
One with, with the actual market problems
of, of a large

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:05.040
business house that had operated very
traditionally and, and

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:12.290
the other was internal to the company of,
of several titans,

00:03:12.290 --> 00:03:17.450
if you might, who, who didn't want any
change to take place,

00:03:17.450 --> 00:03:20.120
and I guess a certain amount of resentment
also.

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:29.335
So, the first five years were akin to
Clint Eastwood if you might, which,

00:03:29.335 --> 00:03:36.320
which tended to be filled with trying to
get these elderly,

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:38.820
very prominent people out of, out of the
way.

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:41.708
It was not done with bullets.

00:03:41.708 --> 00:03:42.441
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:03:42.441 --> 00:03:47.910
&gt;&gt; It was done with persuasion and, and
I'm sorry to say with money.

00:03:49.970 --> 00:03:54.240
So the first five years were spent in
creating an environment where

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:55.430
change was possible.

00:03:57.030 --> 00:04:00.540
And then I've been blessed with an
organization that has been

00:04:01.580 --> 00:04:05.590
displaying considerable spirit in wanting
to make a change.

00:04:07.210 --> 00:04:14.020
And many things that we did had not been
done before but I, I was

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:19.390
always pleased to see that the response
from the organization was tremendous.

00:04:19.390 --> 00:04:21.150
Without that, it could not have been done.

00:04:22.630 --> 00:04:24.480
&gt;&gt; And one of the things that many of us
here,

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:28.700
as students, we study is the inertia of
organizations in the face of change.

00:04:29.830 --> 00:04:34.212
How did you go to inspire the leaders and
the front line staff to, to believe in

00:04:34.212 --> 00:04:38.743
the vision that you set out and some of
the changes that you wished to implement?

00:04:38.743 --> 00:04:44.690
&gt;&gt; That was difficult and to some extent I
would have to humbly say that I failed.

00:04:44.690 --> 00:04:49.850
One, one of the first promises I made
publicly was

00:04:49.850 --> 00:04:54.260
that we'd rationalize the group and reduce
the number of businesses and

00:04:54.260 --> 00:04:56.080
reduce the number of companies.

00:04:57.940 --> 00:05:02.990
I often talk about the first thing that I
did with great gusto, which was to

00:05:02.990 --> 00:05:07.700
get us out of the soaps and toiletry
business, which didn't seem to fit.

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:14.312
And I ran headlong into an issue of what
were we doing.

00:05:14.312 --> 00:05:18.023
We were getting rid of a, a company that
had second and

00:05:18.023 --> 00:05:22.810
third generation employees it was an
unheard of thing, and

00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:27.420
who is this person who is making change
for the sake of change?

00:05:27.420 --> 00:05:27.920
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:05:28.930 --> 00:05:33.100
&gt;&gt; And I got attacked from all, all
quarters.

00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:37.360
The media attacked me, the, the stock
market attacked me,

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:40.790
and of course there was the resentment
from within, within the,

00:05:40.790 --> 00:05:45.200
the company as to which company would
follow now after this.

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:52.960
The net result was a new chairman so keen
to succeed,

00:05:54.220 --> 00:05:58.910
and not wishing to have failure suddenly
being bombarded from all quarters.

00:06:00.380 --> 00:06:04.096
There was no second company that got, got
sold.

00:06:04.096 --> 00:06:05.859
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:06:05.859 --> 00:06:09.265
&gt;&gt; And I put my tail between my legs and

00:06:09.265 --> 00:06:15.836
and tried to look at other forms of of
growing.

00:06:15.836 --> 00:06:16.880
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:20.680
And you've said in the past that risk is a
necessary part of a business

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:25.190
philosophy and certainly of your career
you've made some significant bets, and

00:06:25.190 --> 00:06:27.210
the Dean mentioned a number of the
acquisitions.

00:06:27.210 --> 00:06:29.400
I would also think here of the launch of
the,

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:33.380
the Indica Indians first indigenously
manufactured motor vehicle.

00:06:34.430 --> 00:06:37.650
How did you get comfortable making such
consequential decisions?

00:06:37.650 --> 00:06:43.501
And to your point, convincing others to,
to follow you into them?

00:06:43.501 --> 00:06:48.303
&gt;&gt; I think convincing myself that it would
work, was, is,

00:06:48.303 --> 00:06:54.805
is a judgement call in terms of the faith
you put in your people to do this,

00:06:54.805 --> 00:07:00.109
in case in point was on the Indica
conventional wisdom said

00:07:00.109 --> 00:07:06.515
that you couldn't enter the car business
without having a collaboration,

00:07:06.515 --> 00:07:11.419
and certainly to think of designing a car
domestically and

00:07:11.419 --> 00:07:14.660
producing it was an unheard of thing.

00:07:14.660 --> 00:07:20.585
My friends, as we launched the car,
started to distance themselves from me.

00:07:20.585 --> 00:07:21.870
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Right.

00:07:21.870 --> 00:07:25.290
&gt;&gt; And it wasn't for me, it was to
distance themselves from failure.

00:07:25.290 --> 00:07:30.520
&gt;&gt; Right.
&gt;&gt; And it was the trust one had

00:07:30.520 --> 00:07:35.020
in the young, young team to put this
together that drove it.

00:07:37.230 --> 00:07:39.630
The, the other is that from time to time,

00:07:39.630 --> 00:07:43.290
I think change has caused a lot of people
in the company to, to

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:49.770
question the wisdom that such a change
would, would be based on.

00:07:50.870 --> 00:07:52.740
&gt;&gt; Right.
&gt;&gt; And I've been lucky, and

00:07:52.740 --> 00:07:58.450
in some cases it's been difficult, but by
and large, India is a country

00:07:58.450 --> 00:08:04.490
that has such a great potential that
change is, by and large, well received.

00:08:04.490 --> 00:08:04.990
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:10.950
And we've heard a lot of anxiety about the
Indian economy, and

00:08:10.950 --> 00:08:14.880
we know that there's a lot of potential
there, but there's been recently, at

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:18.795
least, a lot of noise in the press about
how reform hasn't kept pace with growth.

00:08:18.795 --> 00:08:19.650
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.
&gt;&gt; And it's

00:08:19.650 --> 00:08:21.800
possibly cramping India's growth
potential.

00:08:22.860 --> 00:08:25.160
What do you think lies ahead for, for
future of India?

00:08:26.360 --> 00:08:30.690
&gt;&gt; Well, you know, I, I, I've always been
very positive about the new India and

00:08:31.710 --> 00:08:36.950
I've been saddened by the fact that after
several years

00:08:36.950 --> 00:08:42.750
of very progressive growth there's been a
stumbling

00:08:44.150 --> 00:08:53.370
activity that has been a country driven by
accusations of corruption.

00:08:53.370 --> 00:08:54.990
Not wrong, rightly so.

00:08:56.900 --> 00:09:02.020
Crony capitalism and a government that has

00:09:02.020 --> 00:09:07.440
withdrawn its pace of, of growth.

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:08.900
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:09:08.900 --> 00:09:14.050
&gt;&gt; At the same time, I, I feel that, that
has been, that has bottomed out,

00:09:14.050 --> 00:09:16.190
and we're on a path again to grow.

00:09:16.190 --> 00:09:17.880
But we've got to rebuild.

00:09:17.880 --> 00:09:23.670
We've gone from an eight, 8% plus growth
rate, down to sub five briefly.

00:09:23.670 --> 00:09:24.590
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:09:24.590 --> 00:09:28.230
&gt;&gt; We will grow back to the growth rate we
had.

00:09:28.230 --> 00:09:31.550
We will need to control the inflation, but
I think India will,

00:09:31.550 --> 00:09:36.520
once again, the same spirit of the people
will make it, make it happen.

00:09:36.520 --> 00:09:37.620
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:39.985
And one of the things that, that you
mentioned is,

00:09:39.985 --> 00:09:42.185
that's often remarked about the Indian and
for

00:09:42.185 --> 00:09:45.983
that matter a number of emerging markets
is this phenomenon of crony capitalism.

00:09:45.983 --> 00:09:49.943
And you've spoken in the past about how
you value personal integrity,

00:09:49.943 --> 00:09:53.580
both in your own leadership but in also in
the leaders you admire.

00:09:54.940 --> 00:09:58.970
Have you ever faced an opportunity which
you had to turn down for,

00:09:58.970 --> 00:10:03.410
for ethical reasons or for the ethical
consequences of that decision?

00:10:03.410 --> 00:10:08.080
&gt;&gt; Sev, several several instances with,
where that could be so,

00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:13.300
I think perhaps the, the most visible one
was

00:10:16.670 --> 00:10:21.720
when we wanted to form an airline oddly
enough,

00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:26.010
we were asked to form an airline by, by
the then prime minister.

00:10:27.590 --> 00:10:29.170
But vested interests made,

00:10:29.170 --> 00:10:34.149
made sure that we didn't form an airline
through three successive prime ministers.

00:10:35.780 --> 00:10:40.970
And that was the most visible of, of the
thing which we walked away from but

00:10:40.970 --> 00:10:45.830
because we could have probably had it by
succumbing to

00:10:45.830 --> 00:10:50.562
subjective issues of bribery or corruption
and we walked away from it.

00:10:50.562 --> 00:10:51.085
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:10:51.085 --> 00:10:55.570
&gt;&gt; Where airline would have been something
we really wanted to do.

00:10:55.570 --> 00:11:00.010
We pioneered aviation in, at the turn of
the century.

00:11:00.010 --> 00:11:07.560
And it was emotionally something that we
would have liked very much to be in again.

00:11:07.560 --> 00:11:08.880
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:11:08.880 --> 00:11:11.784
I know both you and, and your uncle
[UNKNOWN] were both

00:11:11.784 --> 00:11:16.550
aviation enthusiasts so, I can imagine the
disappointment that you must have felt.

00:11:18.810 --> 00:11:21.350
One of the, one of the hallmarks of your,

00:11:21.350 --> 00:11:25.420
your tenures here has in fact been the
move away from, from India and

00:11:25.420 --> 00:11:29.470
transforming Tata from an India business
into a true multinational.

00:11:30.800 --> 00:11:32.760
What's prompted the move abroad and, and

00:11:32.760 --> 00:11:36.020
what were some of the biggest challenges
you faced in doing so?

00:11:37.030 --> 00:11:44.460
&gt;&gt; To agree to extent it was the, the
issue of having the need to grow and

00:11:44.460 --> 00:11:50.320
a need to take a view that you'd grown in
India in some cases with a fairly

00:11:50.320 --> 00:11:55.280
substantial market share, and that as a
group we ought to look beyond the shores

00:11:55.280 --> 00:12:01.600
of India to another economic cycle in
which we would operate.

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:08.850
Now that, it, it's pluses and minuses that
we didn't ever foresee that Europe would

00:12:08.850 --> 00:12:14.590
go through the downturn that it did, and
that was where our major investments lay.

00:12:15.938 --> 00:12:22.440
So it was a quest for growth and, and
changing the ground rules

00:12:22.440 --> 00:12:26.950
to say that we could grow by acquisitions
which surely we had never done.

00:12:26.950 --> 00:12:27.450
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:12:28.830 --> 00:12:34.030
The international expansion though was not
without some controversy, and

00:12:34.030 --> 00:12:38.140
aside from the economic rationale there
was also emotional consequences and

00:12:38.140 --> 00:12:40.732
I'm thinking here of the acquisition of
Tetley Tea.

00:12:40.732 --> 00:12:41.490
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:12:41.490 --> 00:12:46.230
&gt;&gt; The idea of a beloved British tea brand
being bought by an Indian Company must

00:12:46.230 --> 00:12:47.718
have stirred some hearts in England.

00:12:47.718 --> 00:12:49.504
And-

00:12:49.504 --> 00:12:53.925
&gt;&gt; If, if they did it was quite quiet and
dignified.

00:12:53.925 --> 00:12:56.105
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; The more vocal language.

00:12:56.105 --> 00:12:57.250
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:12:57.250 --> 00:13:01.890
&gt;&gt; The more vocal bone was when Jaguar and
Land Rover got acquired.

00:13:01.890 --> 00:13:03.170
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:13:03.170 --> 00:13:05.600
&gt;&gt; And that was far more emotional and

00:13:05.600 --> 00:13:13.077
far more visible different, different
reactions from different quarters.

00:13:13.077 --> 00:13:20.180
Some in, some concerned that an Indian
company was going to take over

00:13:21.440 --> 00:13:27.770
vaulted two very venerable brands what
were they going to do with it?

00:13:27.770 --> 00:13:28.360
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:13:28.360 --> 00:13:31.920
&gt;&gt; Ford had made, had not been able to
make a profit with them,

00:13:31.920 --> 00:13:34.780
so what was this Indian company that had

00:13:34.780 --> 00:13:40.757
no experience in the premium car segment
do with such a company?

00:13:40.757 --> 00:13:45.950
Concerned by the workers that we were
going to close down plants and

00:13:45.950 --> 00:13:48.460
move them to India.

00:13:48.460 --> 00:13:54.280
And a general feeling that in England that
another British brand was gone.

00:13:55.460 --> 00:13:55.960
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:13:58.870 --> 00:14:03.960
&gt;&gt; Quite frankly, all we did after we got
involved was to tell the management of

00:14:03.960 --> 00:14:07.760
the company to make their own destiny that
we'd support them in terms of

00:14:07.760 --> 00:14:09.240
what they wanted to do.

00:14:09.240 --> 00:14:16.032
And of course the marketplace has been a
very conducive to what we have done.

00:14:16.032 --> 00:14:17.754
Right.

00:14:17.754 --> 00:14:24.020
&gt;&gt; So I, I think the Jaguar, Land Rover
thing was much more fiery than the teeth

00:14:24.020 --> 00:14:30.300
then there's gulp very, very quietly and
then I as said in a very dignified way.

00:14:30.300 --> 00:14:32.770
&gt;&gt; And I doubt the, the British stiff
upper lip was-

00:14:32.770 --> 00:14:33.270
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:14:33.270 --> 00:14:33.770
&gt;&gt; What they applied there.

00:14:33.770 --> 00:14:35.730
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:14:35.730 --> 00:14:40.150
&gt;&gt; One of the remarkable aspects of Tata
is its sheer complexity.

00:14:40.150 --> 00:14:43.470
It strikes me as being an enormously
difficult challenge to be able to

00:14:43.470 --> 00:14:45.680
manage this company day-to-day.

00:14:45.680 --> 00:14:49.640
You have, as the Dean mentioned, almost
half a million employees worldwide,

00:14:49.640 --> 00:14:53.370
over 100 operating companies, 36 of which
are, are publicly listed.

00:14:54.440 --> 00:14:58.290
Dominic Barton of Mackenzie when he was
here this week spoke about great leaders

00:14:58.290 --> 00:15:02.590
needing to have both a microscope and a
telescope that has an ability to focus on

00:15:02.590 --> 00:15:06.480
the fine details where they really count,
but also to see the larger picture and

00:15:06.480 --> 00:15:09.500
see the secular trends transforming their
markets.

00:15:09.500 --> 00:15:14.032
How did you go about managing Tata day to
day and balance that need for a micro and

00:15:14.032 --> 00:15:15.345
a macro perspective?

00:15:15.345 --> 00:15:16.379
&gt;&gt; I think by and

00:15:16.379 --> 00:15:23.260
large all our companies have had strong
CEOs that have run the businesses.

00:15:23.260 --> 00:15:26.390
And the only,

00:15:26.390 --> 00:15:31.818
only thing I would say is that I've had a
funny type of role where you,

00:15:31.818 --> 00:15:38.500
you sit apart looking at the overall
company on a strategic basis.

00:15:38.500 --> 00:15:43.730
And then when there's a problem you go
into the company much more than may

00:15:43.730 --> 00:15:48.920
happen elsewhere in the world, where you
find yourself playing the same role

00:15:48.920 --> 00:15:54.147
as the CEO in terms of either firefighting
or,

00:15:54.147 --> 00:15:59.750
or moving a company in another direction
then move back again into a macro

00:15:59.750 --> 00:16:05.280
position where you're looking at the
company or the group in its broader sense.

00:16:05.280 --> 00:16:05.920
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:16:05.920 --> 00:16:11.590
&gt;&gt; So it, exactly what you said, is
inadvertently has been what,

00:16:11.590 --> 00:16:14.040
what has been done.

00:16:14.040 --> 00:16:17.080
&gt;&gt; And how did you make sure that you had
the information you needed to

00:16:17.080 --> 00:16:18.490
make the decisions?

00:16:18.490 --> 00:16:21.640
So we've had past VFTT CEOs come in and
speak about how

00:16:21.640 --> 00:16:26.680
they would covertly conduct visits to the
front line and, and visit factory floors,

00:16:26.680 --> 00:16:30.620
just to get the, to gauge the spirit and
moral of their workforce.

00:16:30.620 --> 00:16:33.230
&gt;&gt; I think that, that's an absolute given.

00:16:34.350 --> 00:16:39.520
The worst thing that can happen is to, is
to determine where the company is

00:16:39.520 --> 00:16:44.594
going through the eyes or, or the mouth of
one person, I get the CEO.

00:16:44.594 --> 00:16:47.390
&gt;&gt; Right
&gt;&gt; Far more is gained by walking on

00:16:47.390 --> 00:16:51.500
the shelf floor and talking to the people
and trying to communicate.

00:16:51.500 --> 00:16:56.745
One of my weaknesses' has been I think
I've never communicated in there for

00:16:56.745 --> 00:16:59.000
adequately with our people.

00:16:59.000 --> 00:17:04.220
Partly because I'm, I'm a shy person, and

00:17:04.220 --> 00:17:10.140
secondly because I didn't realize the
importance of communication,

00:17:10.140 --> 00:17:14.470
but I think communication with the
employees is an exceedingly important

00:17:15.730 --> 00:17:21.070
function that any company chairman has to,
has to do and be visible in that sense.

00:17:21.070 --> 00:17:24.460
&gt;&gt; Did you ever have to overrule, or

00:17:24.460 --> 00:17:28.840
disagree with decisions made by some of
the leaders of your businesses based on

00:17:28.840 --> 00:17:30.990
information you were hearing from, from
the front lines?

00:17:30.990 --> 00:17:32.810
&gt;&gt; Oh yes, yes, very much so.

00:17:32.810 --> 00:17:38.510
And that, that's usually quite an eye
opener because you have a trusted CEO, and

00:17:38.510 --> 00:17:40.840
you find either inadvertently or

00:17:40.840 --> 00:17:45.190
consciously you're getting a colored
picture of, of a situation.

00:17:45.190 --> 00:17:45.690
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:17:48.590 --> 00:17:53.370
Another one of the approaches that you
took at Tata which is a hallmark of,

00:17:53.370 --> 00:17:56.830
of your tenure, is the focus on the bottom
of the pyramid consumer.

00:17:57.850 --> 00:18:00.410
And we all know the story of the, the Tata
Nano and,

00:18:00.410 --> 00:18:04.730
and, a number of us visited your factory
last year, in, in Gujarat.

00:18:04.730 --> 00:18:08.325
And I was pleasantly surprised to find
that I could fit quite comfortably inside.

00:18:08.325 --> 00:18:11.285
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I'm glad you did.

00:18:11.285 --> 00:18:13.550
[LAUGH]

00:18:13.550 --> 00:18:15.890
&gt;&gt; Well I heard it was modeled after,
after you, so the-

00:18:15.890 --> 00:18:16.680
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].
&gt;&gt; Someone of

00:18:16.680 --> 00:18:18.350
your stature needed to fit comfortably.

00:18:20.220 --> 00:18:24.730
But the, the nano as we spoke about before
the interview has also met with some,

00:18:24.730 --> 00:18:28.590
some challenges and, so I'm curious
whether you're still as

00:18:28.590 --> 00:18:31.700
enthusiastic about the bottom of the
pyramid market today.

00:18:31.700 --> 00:18:34.460
And what lessons did you take from the,
the nano episode?

00:18:34.460 --> 00:18:35.550
&gt;&gt; Good, good question.

00:18:36.786 --> 00:18:40.092
You know the, the Indian market,

00:18:40.092 --> 00:18:46.824
the known consumer market's about 250 to
300 million people.

00:18:46.824 --> 00:18:50.008
If you take the base of the pyramid and

00:18:50.008 --> 00:18:55.042
look at, at developing products below that
and into the,

00:18:55.042 --> 00:19:01.619
into the segment of the market that's
moving into that consuming era area,

00:19:01.619 --> 00:19:09.047
the market size extends to 500 to 600
million people and that's a huge market.

00:19:09.047 --> 00:19:10.535
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:19:10.535 --> 00:19:14.540
&gt;&gt; 300 of which you don't have products to
address.

00:19:15.800 --> 00:19:24.970
So if you can think of how to, how to
reach that, how to reach that market,

00:19:24.970 --> 00:19:30.590
you have an exceedingly large potential
market.

00:19:30.590 --> 00:19:37.960
And case and point might be the, the cell
phone industry in India.

00:19:39.050 --> 00:19:44.830
When I grew up in, in school, you waited
seven to ten years to get a telephone.

00:19:44.830 --> 00:19:49.710
There was a government incumbent company
or

00:19:49.710 --> 00:19:55.190
government monopoly that, that had
limitations to refuse to grow.

00:19:55.190 --> 00:19:56.000
Rates were high.

00:19:57.820 --> 00:20:04.030
So apart from people like me a little
shopkeeper or,

00:20:04.030 --> 00:20:07.800
or a, a handcart puller let's say,

00:20:07.800 --> 00:20:13.920
could never dream of having connectivity
other than through a pay phone.

00:20:16.263 --> 00:20:18.863
&gt;&gt; Right.
&gt;&gt; Today with cell phones,

00:20:18.863 --> 00:20:21.318
it's bypassed all of that.

00:20:21.318 --> 00:20:25.718
And, and the handcart pusher can buy

00:20:25.718 --> 00:20:32.020
a prepaid SIM card and a low cost phone
and he's connected.

00:20:32.020 --> 00:20:34.270
He has a number, he can be reached.

00:20:34.270 --> 00:20:38.360
He has an identity and it's self esteem.

00:20:38.360 --> 00:20:38.980
&gt;&gt; Right.
&gt;&gt; So you have

00:20:41.270 --> 00:20:47.740
cell phone subscribers that are maybe 600
million in India, and

00:20:47.740 --> 00:20:50.910
the fastest growing industry that the
country has seen.

00:20:52.180 --> 00:20:56.750
So, it has come, because technology has
made a phone available, and

00:20:56.750 --> 00:21:01.800
it's no longer dependent on copper wires,
or, or optical fiber.

00:21:01.800 --> 00:21:04.020
And, and this has happened.

00:21:05.220 --> 00:21:08.820
Again, who would have thought, ten years
ago,

00:21:08.820 --> 00:21:13.790
that you would have 500 million telephone
subscribers in India.

00:21:15.000 --> 00:21:20.020
So I think the potential of the Indian
market is huge

00:21:20.020 --> 00:21:23.890
if you address yourself to, to that.

00:21:23.890 --> 00:21:26.680
I don't think the nano adequately did
that.

00:21:26.680 --> 00:21:31.960
And we, the lesson we learned, is you
can't do something on the basis of

00:21:31.960 --> 00:21:37.410
trying to sell it the same way as you
would sell another car.

00:21:37.410 --> 00:21:42.710
You should have been selling it like one
sold a scooter or a motorcycle.

00:21:44.820 --> 00:21:46.590
And instead of two wheels,

00:21:46.590 --> 00:21:50.150
give them four wheels and a safe form of
family transport.

00:21:50.150 --> 00:21:57.846
So we, we learned our lessons on that and
we rectify that as, as we go forward.

00:21:57.846 --> 00:22:01.780
&gt;&gt; Right, so, important profit motives,
but

00:22:01.780 --> 00:22:05.400
also social consequences of, of really
addressing that market.

00:22:05.400 --> 00:22:06.153
&gt;&gt; Yes.
&gt;&gt; And

00:22:06.153 --> 00:22:12.214
Tata's a remarkable record of its
involvement with philanthropic causes, and

00:22:12.214 --> 00:22:18.092
how do you view the role of organizations
like Tata, and in particular the role

00:22:18.092 --> 00:22:23.373
of corporate social responsibility in
addressing needs in society?

00:22:23.373 --> 00:22:25.241
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:22:25.241 --> 00:22:31.318
&gt;&gt; Well I think this, this transience to
personal life also,

00:22:31.318 --> 00:22:37.090
you got a country with tremendous
disparity of income.

00:22:37.090 --> 00:22:41.570
You've got very rich people on the one
hand, and abject poverty on the other.

00:22:45.380 --> 00:22:52.760
You have to be sensitive to, to the fact
that this gap cannot continue to widen

00:22:54.450 --> 00:23:00.110
while there's a large discontented number
of people

00:23:02.130 --> 00:23:07.560
who are looking at the inequities of, of
the environment as they see it.

00:23:09.650 --> 00:23:15.210
To, for companies, therefore, I think
there has to be a, a sensitivity

00:23:15.210 --> 00:23:19.680
to doing something in the, in the
communities around which you operate.

00:23:21.130 --> 00:23:28.080
And in making an effort to, to raise the
quality of life so

00:23:28.080 --> 00:23:33.690
that there's, there's not envy and, and
distaste for

00:23:33.690 --> 00:23:39.730
what was there, but look, the company's
looked at in a more positive sense and

00:23:39.730 --> 00:23:42.380
in enhancing the quality of life of the
people around them.

00:23:43.420 --> 00:23:47.770
We expend about 4% of our net, net profit

00:23:49.250 --> 00:23:54.820
in these rural development activities and
we do it ourselves, and

00:23:56.650 --> 00:24:01.340
someone may argue that this is
shareholder's money, which it is.

00:24:03.040 --> 00:24:10.780
But I think it is part a sense of, of not
just tolerance but

00:24:10.780 --> 00:24:16.240
a sense of welcoming our companies in
wherever they've been.

00:24:17.360 --> 00:24:21.120
And enhancing whatever we've been trying
to do in the product area or

00:24:21.120 --> 00:24:25.290
the services area to community or being
part of the community.

00:24:25.290 --> 00:24:30.550
&gt;&gt; Would you have viewed the role of
corporate social responsibility as

00:24:30.550 --> 00:24:34.190
being quite different in the United States
versus a emerging market like India?

00:24:36.070 --> 00:24:38.840
&gt;&gt; I, I don't know enough about the United
States to make

00:24:38.840 --> 00:24:44.060
a comment but I think in one form or
another it,

00:24:44.060 --> 00:24:50.220
it becomes an issue that all corporation
will have to, have to deal with.

00:24:50.220 --> 00:24:57.099
And, and we had think as, as businessmen
have to consider ourselves

00:24:58.970 --> 00:25:03.280
and removing the image of being the ugly
businessman, the venal businessman, and

00:25:03.280 --> 00:25:10.722
being heads of, of companies and leaders
who's

00:25:10.722 --> 00:25:17.370
interest go beyond that of their company
to the, the communities they serve.

00:25:19.010 --> 00:25:24.030
And if I think if we can do that, we can
undo an image that has been created over

00:25:24.030 --> 00:25:32.820
the last century of greed and, and
self-serving desires.

00:25:32.820 --> 00:25:36.733
So, I think it's something that we need to
be sensitive about.

00:25:36.733 --> 00:25:39.302
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:25:39.302 --> 00:25:42.645
&gt;&gt; So Stanford has a reputation for
entrepreneurship, and

00:25:42.645 --> 00:25:47.380
many of the students here in the crowd
have aspirations to be entrepreneurs.

00:25:47.380 --> 00:25:51.490
Do you think the innovation taking place
in emerging markets is going to

00:25:51.490 --> 00:25:54.870
be driven more by multinationals like
Tata?

00:25:54.870 --> 00:25:59.940
Or do individual entrepreneurs have the
ability to disrupt major markets in,

00:25:59.940 --> 00:26:02.250
in India and others like it?

00:26:02.250 --> 00:26:03.950
&gt;&gt; I would hope that it's the latter.

00:26:03.950 --> 00:26:10.440
I would hope that we can home grow
enterprises in, in various countries.

00:26:11.530 --> 00:26:15.720
I think what the United States has had,
which is very different,

00:26:15.720 --> 00:26:21.070
is its had a an environment where a good
idea driven by

00:26:21.070 --> 00:26:27.160
people of merit has a better chance of
being supported and financed and,

00:26:27.160 --> 00:26:32.410
and nurtured than you might find in, in
developed countries.

00:26:33.700 --> 00:26:38.816
Very often a good idea dies in, in a
developed country or even, I mean

00:26:38.816 --> 00:26:44.567
a developing country or even a developed
country outside the United States.

00:26:44.567 --> 00:26:49.410
And, and that person finally moves to the
United States, and makes a success.

00:26:49.410 --> 00:26:53.490
And it's because the environment allows
that to happen.

00:26:55.410 --> 00:27:01.170
So I think there's a need for an, a
supporting environment to take place, but

00:27:02.270 --> 00:27:07.320
I would hope that we would see more and
more local companies being

00:27:08.460 --> 00:27:14.570
entrepreneurial and creative and, and
being, at some stage, global player.

00:27:14.570 --> 00:27:15.720
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:27:15.720 --> 00:27:19.070
And certainly we've seen a number of
countries try to replicate the success of

00:27:19.070 --> 00:27:23.780
Silicon Valley and have found that it
takes a unique mix of ingredients.

00:27:23.780 --> 00:27:25.340
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Whether it's human capital,

00:27:25.340 --> 00:27:27.030
legal institutions and so on.

00:27:28.290 --> 00:27:30.270
Are you hopeful that India will be able
to,

00:27:30.270 --> 00:27:33.170
to create that dynamic within its own
country?

00:27:33.170 --> 00:27:35.310
&gt;&gt; I think the potential exists.

00:27:35.310 --> 00:27:41.560
Now whether India does or doesn't will
depend, unfortunately, to a much greater

00:27:41.560 --> 00:27:46.900
extent on, on government policy than it
does in a totally free environment.

00:27:50.070 --> 00:27:54.620
&gt;&gt; I mention when you took over that many,
many external observers there thought

00:27:54.620 --> 00:27:56.830
you had large shoes to fill in taking over
from your uncle.

00:27:57.980 --> 00:27:59.870
Now, with another transition of power,

00:27:59.870 --> 00:28:04.070
Cyrus Pallonji Mistry has his own large
shoes to fill, that of yourself.

00:28:05.350 --> 00:28:07.690
I wonder what advice you would give Cyrus,
as he takes,

00:28:07.690 --> 00:28:09.530
takes on his task as chairman?

00:28:13.060 --> 00:28:16.960
&gt;&gt; I've told Cyrus that he needs to be his
own person.

00:28:16.960 --> 00:28:20.270
that, finally, it was something I did.

00:28:20.270 --> 00:28:24.160
I, I realized the shoes I had to fill were
far too big for

00:28:24.160 --> 00:28:27.150
me to mimic the person I wa, I was
following.

00:28:28.630 --> 00:28:32.700
And I just decided that just being myself
and

00:28:32.700 --> 00:28:35.825
do what I thought was right would be the
way to go.

00:28:35.825 --> 00:28:38.270
I've told Cyrus the same thing.

00:28:38.270 --> 00:28:45.770
I think he, he should feel that he is
being his own person and

00:28:45.770 --> 00:28:51.870
let his personality and his conscience, if
you might, drive him.

00:28:54.710 --> 00:28:58.470
&gt;&gt; In a moment I'm going to turn it over
to the audience for, for questions.

00:28:58.470 --> 00:29:00.540
But one last question before we do.

00:29:01.810 --> 00:29:06.430
In Stanford we ask one question of all our
applicants to the MBA program, which is,

00:29:06.430 --> 00:29:07.760
what matters most to you and why?

00:29:09.460 --> 00:29:12.561
I wonder how you'd answer that question.

00:29:12.561 --> 00:29:14.120
[LAUGH].

00:29:14.120 --> 00:29:15.830
&gt;&gt; I'd obviously fail at Stanford,

00:29:15.830 --> 00:29:18.520
because I don't know how I can answer that
question.

00:29:18.520 --> 00:29:26.110
I, to me I think short term the transition
of suddenly having a lot of time and

00:29:26.110 --> 00:29:31.560
being able to be the master of that time
is, is a lovely luxury.

00:29:33.220 --> 00:29:38.190
Whether that luxury becomes boredom as
time goes on, is

00:29:38.190 --> 00:29:43.580
something that will need to be seen, and
how it gets filled with other pursuits.

00:29:45.100 --> 00:29:51.000
As head of the Trust, I, I will be
involved in some of the things we've

00:29:51.000 --> 00:29:56.160
talked about in the past few minutes, of
trying to make a difference in,

00:29:56.160 --> 00:30:01.510
in what in the quality of life of the
people in, in India.

00:30:01.510 --> 00:30:08.290
But to what extent, my next few years will
lead,

00:30:08.290 --> 00:30:13.300
lead me to fulfill that or change from
that view, I don't know.

00:30:14.500 --> 00:30:20.380
So, with humility, I'd have to say, I
wouldn't volunteer the success

00:30:20.380 --> 00:30:25.530
mode the Stanford graduate would in
answering your question.

00:30:25.530 --> 00:30:29.231
&gt;&gt; Well, I think of a lifetime of, of
service that we,

00:30:29.231 --> 00:30:31.300
we can draw any conclusions to.

00:30:31.300 --> 00:30:32.990
Some of the things that have clearly
mattered to

00:30:32.990 --> 00:30:35.364
you in terms of the impact that you've had
on, on Indian society.

00:30:35.364 --> 00:30:38.754
&gt;&gt; I think if, if, I think seriously if I
said I

00:30:38.754 --> 00:30:44.806
would like to be seen as someone who could
continue to try to make a difference,

00:30:44.806 --> 00:30:49.690
I think that would be a very satisfying
thing for me to achieve.

00:30:51.400 --> 00:30:54.700
&gt;&gt; I think that's a great point to, to
turn it over to questions.

00:30:54.700 --> 00:30:57.690
&gt;&gt; A question we have been struggling with
in some of our classes is

00:30:57.690 --> 00:31:01.100
the role that large companies have in
creating macro change, and

00:31:01.100 --> 00:31:02.820
you're clearly doing that in India.

00:31:02.820 --> 00:31:06.160
I'm wondering if you, if you have any
anecdotes of ways in which you've been

00:31:06.160 --> 00:31:10.470
able, by your position, to influence the
government to make certain decisions that

00:31:10.470 --> 00:31:13.460
were in the best interest of the country
when maybe they wouldn't have?

00:31:16.870 --> 00:31:22.800
&gt;&gt; Your, your question relates to how we
are, how we are channeling or

00:31:22.800 --> 00:31:28.440
deciding how, how and where to channel our
funds in, in that sense, or?

00:31:28.440 --> 00:31:32.450
&gt;&gt; Al, almost the, the influence that a
large business, large business that is,

00:31:32.450 --> 00:31:34.250
that has a vision for, for good for

00:31:34.250 --> 00:31:38.660
the country can have in changing or
influencing policy, pol positively?

00:31:43.590 --> 00:31:48.460
&gt;&gt; I, I think I don't know if I'm
answering your

00:31:48.460 --> 00:31:53.561
question adequately but I think large
business has,

00:31:53.561 --> 00:31:58.885
has to have an element of, of orienting
themselves to,

00:31:58.885 --> 00:32:05.721
to serve a social need also in addition to
just serving a business need.

00:32:05.721 --> 00:32:13.857
And in our case, I think we that may be
driven by, by business motivation,

00:32:13.857 --> 00:32:19.022
but we're, we should always be conscious
of,

00:32:19.022 --> 00:32:24.730
of areas that we feel are not in the
public interest.

00:32:26.485 --> 00:32:31.600
And now going into dangerous waters, but

00:32:33.110 --> 00:32:40.060
we withdrew from activities in the, in the
liquor business,

00:32:40.060 --> 00:32:46.520
because we felt that this was an area that
tended to be

00:32:48.380 --> 00:32:54.680
serving a consumer base that we, that
perhaps, we didn't want to be.

00:32:55.770 --> 00:33:03.080
We, we looked very hard about being in, in
certain natural resource business,

00:33:03.080 --> 00:33:08.620
which, which had become rather venal in
terms of how those businesses were run.

00:33:10.460 --> 00:33:16.655
And I think similarly, we've entered
businesses may be small,

00:33:16.655 --> 00:33:22.640
in a small way, which have been what we
considered to be a worthwhile

00:33:22.640 --> 00:33:27.575
thing to do for the communities that we've
been in, but

00:33:27.575 --> 00:33:33.140
I don't think there's, there's been an, an
open and a, and

00:33:33.140 --> 00:33:39.982
a very constructive way to do what you've,
what you've suggested as here.

00:33:39.982 --> 00:33:40.983
[APPLAUSE].

00:33:40.983 --> 00:33:42.952
&gt;&gt; Thank you, Phil, thank, thanks.

00:33:42.952 --> 00:33:45.988
[APPLAUSE]

00:33:45.988 --> 00:33:51.069
[MUSIC]

